# VIctory fork (work in progress)

A work-in-progress fork of [VIctory](https://github.com/Marvisak/VIctory), a
compatibility layer that runs an Xbox Series title natively on Windows from
files the user supplies. This fork follows upstream
`main` and adds fixes aimed at one goal: getting the game's world to render
correctly and playably on one particular PC.

You need your own copy of the game. The two screenshots below show the
compatibility layer's output from a local installation. This project is
independent of the game's developer and publisher. [Asset policy](docs/asset-policy.md)

## Rendering progress on November 25, 2026

The Radeon RX 7900 XTX renders the opening cutscene with the ray-tracing
builder disabled. The latest build captured the character behind reinforced
glass before the memory guard stopped the run. An earlier normal-mode run
reached the first playable room and measured 4.1–4.8 fps there.
With the experimental ray-tracing builder enabled, the opening scene now
runs 131 frames to the end of a 275-second guarded run, without tripping
the invalid-geometry probe or crashing. Lighting in those frames is still
wrong. [Renderer handoff](HANDOFF.md)

The builder's damaged structure headers came from one stage. Its console
program marks the loads and stores it uses to pass data between threads as
coherent, and the translation had not declared the host's views of title
memory that way. On the Radeon that stage wrote child boxes and pointers
over a structure's header in **15 of 3,228 dispatches** across three
guarded runs. With the declaration it did so in **none of 6,762** across
two runs. A whole-header GPU trace around every builder stage located it:
`VICTORY_BUILD_HEADER_TRACE=1`.

An opening-scene trace caught the host copying a size before the GPU
finished computing it. **All 128 sampled copies** picked up the previous
contents of their size slot. In one case, the host copied a size of
1,734,784 bytes for a structure that required 5,220,224. The next
structure began at the smaller size rounded to 256 bytes. A later shader
read a leaf inside that overlap and selected geometry record 342 from a
table of 44.

With the experimental same-list fix, **all 509 completed size copies
across three guarded runs delivered the current result**. The latest run
verified 322 copies. In 298 cases the value differed from the previous
one, two slots repeated the same value, and 22 had no earlier sample.

Before that correction, one run exposed a damaged header before compaction
and a shader selecting record 538 from two. With the correction the
character and vehicles of the opening scene appear in 3D with unlit skin
and overexposed highlights. The refit stage still leaves an unset root box
in 24 of 1,767 dispatches. The images below are the earlier normal-mode
captures.

| Opening cutscene | First playable room |
| --- | --- |
| [![Woman in a red uniform seen through wire-reinforced glass](docs/screenshots/opening-cutscene.jpg)](docs/screenshots/opening-cutscene.jpg) | [![Character facing a sunlit window in a dark room](docs/screenshots/first-gameplay-room.jpg)](docs/screenshots/first-gameplay-room.jpg) |
| Frame 3956 from the integrated build in normal mode. The memory guard stopped this run during the cutscene. | Frame 4300 from an earlier normal-mode run. Character shading and room geometry still show visible errors. |

### Renderer work from DexNom

This revision adapts shader and buffer-view changes from DexNom's Radeon
renderer. They cover sampler selection, wave masks, pixel quad sampling,
cube-map level of detail, ray traversal across guest pages, and root buffer
views whose guest addresses have been reused. We compared the previous
build with the integrated build on nine shaders from a local installation.
Microsoft's DXIL validator accepted **2 of 9 before and 9 of 9 after**.
The seven recovered programs include a terrain mesh shader and a voxel
visibility pixel shader. The sample measures shader translation. A local
run of DexNom's unmodified reference build ended in an access violation
after 186 seconds, before we could reproduce its reported City results.
[DexNom's tested source](https://github.com/DexNom/VIctory/tree/2e01ab4760db91820ef5f9a9f85761f83cd6b5d0)

## Chapter II (this repository)

This repository continues from
[fxceless/victory-fork](https://github.com/fxceless/victory-fork), whose work
is everything described below, and adds testing on an AMD card (Radeon RX
7900 XTX) with a few tools made for that:

- `tools\run-guarded.ps1`: launches the title for a bounded time under a
  watchdog that stops it at a long GPU submission, a silent log, a memory cap
  or the time limit. Add `-MemoryTrace` to record process private bytes and
  working set alongside system commit, free commit and available physical
  memory in a CSV beside the run log.
- `victory translate --only <text>`, `--hash <16 digits>,...` and
  `--by-instruction`: translate only the containers named by label or by the
  hash the GPU layer logs, and count failures by the instruction they stop
  at.

Fixes made here:

- **View indices are clamped on every host.** The base clamped a view index
  a shader computes to a null slot only where the host caps the size of view
  heaps. On a host that does not (this AMD card), an index past the heap
  read whatever memory lies behind it as a descriptor, and the GPU faulted
  at the address found there: the device was lost after 9 and after 15
  minutes in the world, in the skinning and meshlet-culling compute
  programs. Such a host's view heap is now made 2^21 slots long with a null
  last slot, and the same clamp applies.

- **Blending is left out on integer render targets.** A mesh pipeline of
  the world blends into an 8-bit integer target; the host refused the whole
  pipeline (`E_INVALIDARG`, debug layer message 676) and its draws were
  dropped.
- **More per-draw table slots where the view heap has room** (2^19 on a
  host that does not cap view heaps): 2^18 ran out at about 10,000 draws a
  frame, and draws were dropped.

- **Title memory is declared globally coherent where the console program
  asks for it.** The structure builder's bottom-up stage has each thread
  store its node's box, count itself at the parent with an atomic
  operation, and the thread that arrives second read the first one's box.
  The console program marks those loads and stores coherent. A translated
  program with such loads or stores now declares its root views and its
  views of title memory by address globally coherent
  (`VICTORY_COHERENT_MEMORY=0` restores the earlier declaration, `1`
  applies it to every program; the value is in the shader cache key).
  Without it that stage sometimes wrote a node's children at offset 0 of
  the structure, over its header.

- **A builder stage reads a title buffer through the buffer's own copy.**
  The triangle-reload stage fetches each frame's skinned vertices by
  address. The directory of title memory gave it the title's page pool in
  place, while the programs that write those vertices use the host copy of
  the title buffer around them, so it read an earlier frame or zeros. A
  pool page that lies whole inside a title buffer with its own copy is now
  given that copy (`VICTORY_POOL_OVER_BUFFERS=1` restores the earlier
  order). In one run each, unset root boxes after a refit fell from 13 of
  716 to 2 of 538, and a later full-length run had none in 7,699 refits
  over 131 frames with the builder. Lighting is still wrong, and 55 of 602
  structures still get oversized boxes.

- **Memory the layer hands the title is at console addresses.** On the
  console every address a title gets fits 40 bits, and its GPU programs
  keep only those. Three places gave out host heap memory, which on this
  machine lies above 2^40: a command list's upload memory (where the
  ray-tracing structure builder keeps the parameter block of each stage),
  heaps and descriptor heaps made through the device, and the default
  allocator and C runtime heap of the title's modules. All now come from
  reservations below 2^37, which is also as far as the GPU side's directory
  of title memory by address reaches.

Diagnostics added:

- `VICTORY_BACK_LOG=<file>` and `VICTORY_BACK_DUMP=<folder>`: once the
  ray-tracing structure builder runs, each of its dispatches with its root
  views and constants, and each range copied back into title memory with
  the bytes before and after.

- `VICTORY_BUILD_HEADER_TRACE=1` (or a record count; 16,384 by default):
  the whole 64-byte header of a structure, sampled on the GPU just before
  and just after every builder stage that is given the structure's root,
  with the pages that stage's program noted as having no host copy. One
  log line per dispatch, in submission order, once its list has completed.
  `VICTORY_BUILD_HEADER_TRIANGLES=<count>` remains for following the
  structures of one input size through every stage.

- Buffer views left null are counted by reason in the frame report (raw
  view not on 16 bytes, between elements of the host copy, empty, and so
  on), and the first few of each reason are logged.
- The frame report says what became of the pages shaders found empty in
  title memory by address (given a copy now, not the title's memory, no
  number left, and so on).
- `VICTORY_HOST_MAX_WAVE=32` translates shaders as for a card with 32-lane
  waves. The base was only run on such a card; a card with 64-lane waves
  (AMD) runs console wave64 compute programs natively instead, a path no
  card had run. The switch is part of the shader cache key.

**Ray-tracing builder.** Some `DispatchX` calls specify individual threads.
The host used their X count as the number of groups, causing the box-node
programs to overwrite neighboring structures. The layer now converts those
dimensions to groups and masks inactive lanes in the final wave for the
five verified builder programs. On the Radeon, the 64-byte and 128-byte
box programs both preserved the bytes beyond the requested output in
isolated tests. In the opening scene, a triangle-reload lane selected
record **1,321 from a geometry table containing eight**. Three overlapping
structure copies had destination spacings that matched the previous results
in three reused size-query slots. In a second run with asynchronous
submission and copyback disabled, another lane selected **3,732,840 from
44**. Its structure header was already corrupt. The fault persists in both
configurations. The handoff gives the measured sizes and run limits.

A separate 16-byte GPU test reproduced the ordering error seen in the
opening scene. A buffer copy following a GPU readback in the same command
list saw the previous contents. Submitting the copy after completion
returned the expected bytes.

**Page-pool memory.** `VICTORY_INPLACE_POOL=1` opens the title's page pool
through a view using `OpenExistingHeapFromAddress1`. On this AMD system,
the older file-mapping-handle path left about 11 GiB committed after the
process exited. The view path returned system commit to its baseline in
guarded testing. Peak memory use is still high. A run with 1 MiB sized
copy windows added 34.6 GiB of system commit while the process peaked at
24.9 GiB private. The watchdog monitors both process and system limits.

Merged on 2026-11-25 (revision 5), all on the same base commit as this
repository:

- **[fxceless/victory-fork](https://github.com/fxceless/victory-fork)** up
  to `c96b4f2`: the depth bounds test, textures picked by index looked at
  again after their upload, new defaults for copying back, a clamp of view
  indices on every host (taken there from another fork; it replaces the
  one made here, with the same null slot), and the shader work of other
  forks it had collected. This is where most of the gain below comes from.
- **[uiliannn/VItoriaBr](https://github.com/uiliannn/VItoriaBr)** up to
  `d5a7580`: its shader work had mostly reached the base already; its last
  change (interpolation modes reconciled per signature row) is taken from
  it directly. Its renaming of the project and its README are not.
- **[Zettrox/ztrx](https://github.com/Zettrox/ztrx)** `90b529f` was looked
  at: the base as of an earlier commit with its own README and a launcher
  script, nothing to take.

Kept from this repository through the merges: the blend state for integer
render targets, the wider run of per-draw table slots, memory handed to
the title at console addresses, the page pool in place for memory by
address, and the diagnostics.

After the merge, on the AMD card: 4.1 to 4.8 frames per second in the
first gameplay scene (207-244 ms per frame; it was 400-550), the cutscene
before it lit, and the wall around the first room's window drawn. The
shader census: 25,598 programs emitted, 96 not (108 before).

State on the AMD card (2026-11-25, revision 4, before the merge): the opening cutscene and the
first gameplay scene render and can be played, at about 2 frames per second
(400-550 ms per frame), about 25-30 GB of memory, no device loss. It does not
look right:

- Shadowed areas are black: the ray-traced light does not run. With
  `VICTORY_BVH_BUILDER=1` all 53 pipelines of the structure builder get host
  pipelines on this card and the builder runs, which the base reports it
  never did on a 32-lane card; the title then crashes within seconds, on
  bounding-box bit patterns where it keeps pointers. At that revision the
  layer reached title memory by address through numbered host copies of 4 MiB
  windows, at most 1,023 of them. The builder's first frame in the world
  numbers 1,013; from the second frame on hundreds of pages a frame have
  no copy (their reads give 0, their stores are lost), and what is copied
  back into title memory from such a frame is wrong. With the builder's
  stores kept out of title memory (`VICTORY_BVH_QUARANTINE=1`) the title
  runs on, at 5-12 seconds a frame. With four times the copies
  (`VICTORY_MEMORY_COPIES=4095`) nearly 2,000 windows (8 GiB) were
  numbered within three minutes and still more were asked for. Copies do
  not scale to this; the title's memory has to be the card's memory in
  place (`inplace.rs`, of which the base has a first stage).
  Begun (on with `VICTORY_BVH_BUILDER=1` or `VICTORY_INPLACE_POOL=1`): the
  title's page pool is made of committed sections of at most 1 GiB, each
  opened as a host heap with buffers placed over it; the directory of
  title memory by address points every page of the pool at its place
  there as the title maps it (225,000 pages in the first scene, through 9
  numbers, no copy, no lost access); root views of the same shaders are
  bound in place too. The builder's frame then takes about 270 ms where it
  took 600 to 2,000. What such a shader reads and writes through a root
  view goes by the directory too, page by page (`USER_ROOT_ADDRESS`): the
  title maps its pool one page at a time, and a structure of several
  pages is often in no one piece there.
  The title still dies within seconds of the builder's first frame, at a
  different place each run. With the pool in place and the builder off it
  runs as before, so what is left is in what the builder's programs
  store: none of the 53 had run on a card before, and a store at a wrong
  address now lands in the title's memory at once. Earlier runs counted
  100 to 500 pages a frame touched at addresses that are not the title's
  memory at all. Later captures identified the triangle-reload program
  and the invalid geometry records documented in the renderer handoff.
- Walls, floors and terrain are missing in the first gameplay scene, and so
  are parts of characters. No draw is dropped for it and the debug layer
  reports no error at draw time. One cause found, not fixed: a buffer view
  of 12-byte elements that starts neither on 16 bytes nor on an element of
  its host copy cannot be made on the host and reads zeros.
- Exposure swings between near black and white.

